{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n`.rebar/enrich-drain.lock` is an `O_EXCL` existence lock with no owner stamp and no\nstaleness path. `enrich_drain._acquire_advisory_lock` creates it and never writes to the\nfd; `_release_advisory_lock` unlinks it. Any drainer dying between those two calls leaks a\n0-byte file PERMANENTLY: every later drain sees `FileExistsError`, returns `None`, and\n`drain()` logs at INFO and returns `{\"skipped\": \"lock-held\"}` — enrichment stops with no\nerror and no visible signal. The new `rebar doctor` lock-health report (task\n513e-35e1-2c80-4428) found exactly this on this host's live tracker: a 0-byte drain lock\nheld ~26 days with no drain process alive, silently skipping enrichment for a month.\n\n## Approach\nGive the drain lock the ownership discipline the store's mkdir write lock already has, by\nREUSING that machinery rather than writing a third locking dialect.\n\n1. **Stamp on acquire.** After the `O_EXCL` create succeeds, write\n   `lock_owner._owner_stamp()` (the existing v2 `host=/ns=/pid=/start=` line) into the\n   file — best-effort like `lock.py`'s mkdir stamp write, since a failed stamp leaves an\n   unstamped lock that the ceiling still bounds.\n2. **Adjudicate on collision.** Extract the decision table already inside\n   `lock_owner._mkdir_lock_is_stale` into a shared core\n\n       _stamp_is_stale(stamp, artifact_path, *, fcntl_held, unrecognised_via_ceiling)\n\n   taking an already-read stamp plus the path whose mtime measures the hold age.\n   `_mkdir_lock_is_stale` becomes a thin reader that delegates; a new sibling\n   `stamped_file_is_stale(path)` reads the stamp from the FILE and delegates to the same\n   core with `fcntl_held=False`. No new threshold, no new pid probe: the pid-recycle\n   qualification, the refuse-without-proof branches and the 1h\n   `_MKDIR_LOCK_STALE_CEILING_S` backstop are inherited as-is.\n\n   `unrecognised_via_ceiling` is the ONLY behavioural knob; it selects what happens to a\n   stamp that is not v2 (empty or any other unrecognised text), and nothing else in the\n   core branches on it — `fcntl_held` does not and cannot select it.\n\n   - `_mkdir_lock_is_stale` passes `False`, preserving TODAY's routing exactly: non-v2 →\n     `_legacy_stamp_is_stale`. Worked example: an owner file containing `\"\"` parses\n     non-v2, reaches `_legacy_stamp_is_stale(\"\")`, finds no `:`, returns False — the same\n     answer as today, so the mkdir leg is unchanged.\n   - `stamped_file_is_stale` passes `True`: non-v2 is NO PROOF OF OWNERSHIP and falls to\n     the ceiling. Worked example: the leaked 0-byte `enrich-drain.lock`, 26 days old,\n     parses non-v2, takes the ceiling branch, exceeds 3600s, returns True — reclaimable.\n     The same file 5 seconds old is under the ceiling and returns False — respected.\n\n   The asymmetry is load-bearing: the mkdir lock has a real legacy `<host>:<pid>` dialect\n   to honour; the drain lock never had one, so \"unrecognised\" there can only mean\n   \"unstamped orphan\" — the exact pre-fix shape this bug must make reclaimable.\n3. **Bounded, loud reclaim.** On `FileExistsError`, ask `stamped_file_is_stale`. If it\n   refuses, skip as today. If it says stale, log at WARNING naming the holder stamp and\n   the age (mirroring `_reclaim_mkdir_lock`), unlink, and retry the create EXACTLY ONCE;\n   a second `FileExistsError` means another drainer won the race, so we skip. No loop. A\n   live, corroborated holder is never broken because the shared core refuses it.\n4. **Attributable skip.** `drain()`'s skip log moves from INFO to WARNING and names the\n   holder decoded from the stamp, so a wedge is visible without running doctor.\n\nThe drain lock is advisory only (it stops two drain PROCESSES from redundant work; the\nqueue claim is separately optimistic), so a lost reclaim race costs at worst one redundant\ndrain, never correctness — which is why one bounded retry needs no arbitration.\n\n**Prerequisite.** `rebar doctor`'s drain leg reports `not-assessable` from\n`_commands/doctor_locks.py`, a NEW module created by task 513e-35e1-2c80-4428 (Gerrit\n1701, `LLM-Review +1`, awaiting `Verified`), absent from today's tree. This change is\nCOMMITTED to landing as 1701's stacked child — authored on top of 1701's commit, so\n`doctor_locks.py` is a guaranteed edit target and 1701 submits first; if 1701 merges\nfirst the stack collapses to a plain rebase. The edge is `relates_to`, not `depends_on`,\ndeliberately: a blocking edge to a still-open prerequisite makes `review-plan` fast-fail\nwithout running, and 513e is in flight, not waiting.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/_store/lock_owner.py` — extract `_stamp_is_stale` core; add\n  `stamped_file_is_stale`; `_mkdir_lock_is_stale` delegates (behaviour preserved).\n- `src/rebar/llm/enrich_drain.py` — stamp on acquire; stale-reclaim with one bounded\n  retry; holder description helper; WARNING skip log.\n- `src/rebar/_commands/doctor_locks.py` — the drain leg decodes the stamp and reports a\n  real staleness verdict instead of `not-assessable`; `schemas/doctor.schema.json`,\n  `_cli/help/doctor.txt` and `docs/cli-reference.md` follow if its vocabulary changes.\n- `tests/unit/test_enrich_drain.py`, `tests/scripts/graph/test_doctor_locks.py`.\n\n## Testing\nUnit tests in `tests/unit/test_enrich_drain.py` drive `_acquire_advisory_lock`/`drain`\nagainst a lock file planted in each state: stamped-for-a-dead-pid (reclaimed), 0-byte and\nback-dated past the ceiling (reclaimed), 0-byte and fresh (refused — the create/stamp\nwindow), stamped-for-a-live-pid (refused, bytes unchanged, WARNING names the holder), and\nforeign-host (refused while young). One forces every `os.open` to raise `FileExistsError`\nand asserts two attempts; one asserts release still removes the file; one asserts an\nacquired lock's bytes parse as a v2 stamp naming us.\nThe existing lock-owner suites (`test_lock_age_ceiling.py`, `test_lock_stale_reclaim.py`,\n`test_lock_container_recreate.py`) guard the extraction's unchanged behaviour.\n`tests/scripts/graph/test_doctor_locks.py` covers the doctor drain leg: dead-pid stamp →\n`stale-lock` finding + exit 1, live-pid stamp → no finding + exit 0, read-only preserved.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A dead holder no longer wedges enrichment: with a drain lock stamped for a\n      provably-dead pid, a test asserts `drain()` returns a non-skipped result.\n- [ ] A pre-fix unstamped orphan is reclaimed: with a 0-byte lock back-dated past the\n      ceiling, a test asserts acquisition succeeds.\n- [ ] The create/stamp window is respected: with a 0-byte lock created just now, a test\n      asserts acquisition is refused.\n- [ ] A live holder is honoured: with a lock stamped for a live pid, a test asserts\n      `drain()` returns `{\"skipped\": \"lock-held\"}`.\n- [ ] That refused attempt leaves the lock file byte-identical, asserted by comparing its\n      bytes before and after.\n- [ ] An acquired lock carries the existing v2 stamp: a test asserts its contents parse\n      with `lock_owner._parse_v2_stamp` into our own host and pid.\n- [ ] No second staleness heuristic exists: a grep of the diff shows no new age constant,\n      no pid probe deciding staleness outside `lock_owner`.\n- [ ] The reclaim is bounded: with every create raising `FileExistsError`, a test asserts\n      `os.open` was called exactly twice.\n- [ ] The reclaim is loud: `caplog` shows a WARNING naming the reclaimed stamp and age.\n- [ ] A skipped drain is attributable: `caplog` shows a WARNING naming the holder.\n- [ ] `rebar doctor`'s drain leg reports a real staleness verdict rather than\n      `not-assessable`, asserted on the `--output json` row.\n- [ ] A dead-holder drain lock becomes a `stale-lock` finding at exit 1, asserted in\n      `tests/scripts/graph/test_doctor_locks.py`.\n- [ ] Doctor still reclaims nothing: its read-only test passes with the drain lock file\n      unchanged after a run.\n- [ ] Every touched `src/rebar` file stays under the 800-LOC cap, verified by the\n      module-size gate.\n- [ ] The full local gate set is green: `check_*` gates, generators re-run with no diff,\n      `make lint`, `make typecheck`, CI's pytest invocation."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786597375109571001,"uuid":"cc95320e-634b-48af-b6ff-d6602ca29810"}